cal intervention  we always talk about the benefits versus  the risks  so it sounds like a good idea to be  testing kids  um every week every other week to look  for  cases but actually is it a good idea is  the efficacy there  is it proven that actually um then  quarantining those children  is going to be of benefit both to the  child and  also the community transmission  and are the tests good enough for that  purpose and this  really needs to be clarified by the  government and by the scientific  advisors  okay and let's bring in david laws on  this point there's a lot of pressure on  the government to get this right  schools need to stay open now don't they  could this be a useful tool in making  that work  it could certainly be a useful tool but  i agree very much with the two other  guests that we need to think through the  practicalities of this  and it's surprising to me that just  three or four days before  the government plans to announce what  its strategy is for reopening schools on  the 8th of march  there still seem to be quite detailed  discussions and controversies about this  it's going to be an enormous challenge  for schools or for parents if this is  going to go ahead  and really there needs to be more notice  in this area  and in other areas so that schools have  a proper chance to plan  okay not to say it can't happen of  course we've seen haven't we the huge  success of the vaccine rollouts  well let's move on now to our next  question and this is about the crucial  issue of the future of exams we've had a  few on this  let's take a listen do you believe that  it will be fair to make the 2022 exam  cohort set a full examination series  next year  given the impacts on their education  since march of 2020  and if so how would you justify that  with the discrepancies in  education due to digital poverty  what is being done to help the year 11  and year 12s  who have heightened anxiety sitting  exams due to the fact that they weren't  able to sit 