e very good at that  they're very  used to having mock and other  examinations and that will be  a way of allowing students who have  missed gcses  to prepare properly for their a levels  okay so a lot of it will depend on how  quickly we can get  students back into the classroom i want  to bring in rachel and i want to talk  about  the a word not in terms of grades but of  course  algorithms no one wants to see the  return of those do they  no um what we don't want to see is a  repeat of what happened last summer  most of all for the young people who are  waiting for  who are due to take some form of  assessment for their gcse's a level or  vocational qualifications this summer  i think i just go back to david's point  about timeliness  um we haven't had a lot of forward  notice from government  about the changes we've been asked to  manage in schools and colleges  despite that i think we've done a good  job um in terms of being responsive and  agile  but um with reference to a first  question  i would say that we do need some  mitigation for the 2022 exams  because of the differential learning  loss which the current year 10  and year 12 have already suffered and we  really do need to know what that would  look like if we're going to have  some curtail content for exams for  example  before the summer holiday so that  teachers have time to plan  the curriculum appropriately for when  students come back in september  so their experience is measured and they  feel reassured that teachers  always acting their best interests have  had the time to prepare and to make sure  that the work that they're doing is  really going to pay off for them  when it comes to those exams in 2022.  and of course dr ellie i know you'll  have picked up on the word anxiety being  used in that second question there i  know it's an issue as a gp that you're  seeing  with youngsters let's just before i get  some response from you on that  here from 16 year old emily harris she's  got a point 