 many people  who um are experts who talk about  children young people being resilient um  and bouncing back and i think um  the teenager who just asked the question  perfectly illustrates that actually  many young people are not resilient and  we can't just expect them to bounce back  just like we wouldn't expect an adult to  bounce back  it's been one of the big paradoxes of  the whole pandemic hasn't it that the  children who are actually least affected  by this virus have actually had to pay  one of the biggest prices  let's move into our next question now  this is from 13 year old  hinogoto all my cousins living in japan  haven't missed a day of school  japanese schools have remained open  throughout the pandemic  teachers and students there wear face  masks all day long including during  lessons  and lunches taken in classrooms so that  kids don't face one another while eating  here in the uk i'm wondering why we're  only required to wear face masks in the  hallways and not inside the classrooms  too  okay well let's ask david laws his views  on this i mean it's quite extraordinary  to think that their schools have stayed  open throughout  all of this in japan certainly for the  kids here in the uk totally different  experience could we have done things  differently of their lessons to be  learned from our  international neighbours  i think there are certainly a lot of  lessons for us to learn as a country not  just about how we've managed this  in the education system but across the  whole of  society and we looked pretty uh slow  moving as a country early on in the  pandemic to take some of the actions  that we needed to take  it was also controversial obviously how  we should deal with some of the  protective elements around this and as  you know not just in schools but outside  schools the advice on  whether people should wear face masks at  all  changed hugely in the first six months  of the pandemic  so i think that this is a perfectly  sensible question to ask should we be  following other countries  in using face masks more in school  we know that policy has evolved in this  area but that really is  has got to be one for the medical  experts who advise the government on  these matters  and it's important as far as possible  that they that we follow their advice  and look at the latest evidence that  we've got about for example the way  the virus transmits between young people  and the way that the virus translates  outside school between young people and  older people so we need to follow the  medical advice on this  okay it's going to be a work in progress  as well isn't it okay david thanks for  that  let's move on to our next questions  we've got so many i want to make sure we  get through as many as we can  this is from 13 year old india sethi  given the fact we may be going back to  school in three weeks time  when should our wonderful teachers get  vaccinated  oh long may that enthusiasm for teachers  continue hey rachel  do you think that teachers should be  bumped up the priority list  i think it was entirely appropriate that  the vulnerable the elderly and nhs  frontline workers were prioritized  in the first instance of vaccination and  i know many many of my colleagues do in  teaching as well  but we do feel now in the second phase  that vaccinations should be prioritized  by occupation  there are just under a million teaching  and support staff  in schools in england and i think the  risk of those  people working in crowded schools where  there's very little social distancing  possible and in primary schools where  there is no social distancing between  students and staff  is very different to someone perhaps in  their 50s  who's working from home and is not going  to  expose themselves to a similar type of  risk so for me  to give reassurance to staff in our  schools for whom i feel responsible and  there's a duty of care  i really do think that there should be  some urgent thought  given to um prior