 many people  who um are experts who talk about  children young people being resilient um  and bouncing back and i think um  the teenager who just asked the question  perfectly illustrates that actually  many young people are not resilient and  we can't just expect them to bounce back  just like we wouldn't expect an adult to  bounce back  it's been one of the big paradoxes of  the whole pandemic hasn't it that the  children who are actually least affected  by this virus have actually had to pay  one of the biggest prices  let's move into our next question now  this is from 13 year old  hinogoto all my cousins living in japan  haven't missed a day of school  japanese schools have remained open  throughout the pandemic  teachers and students there wear face  masks all day long including during  lessons  and lunches taken in classrooms so that  kids don't face one another while eating  here in the uk i'm wondering why we're  only required to wear face masks in the  hallways and not inside the classrooms  too  okay well let's ask david laws his views  on this i mean it's quite extraordinary  to think that their schools have stayed  open throughout  all of this in japan certainly for the  kids here in the uk totally different  experience could we have done things  differently of their lessons to be  learned from our  international neighbours  i think there are certainly a lot of  lessons for us to learn as a country not  just about how we've managed this  in the education system but across the  whole of  society and we looked pretty uh slow  moving as a country early on in the  pandemic to take some of the actions  that we needed to take  it was also controversial obviously how  we should deal with some of the  protective elements around this and as  you know not just in schools but outside  schools the advice on  whether people should wear face masks at  all  changed hugely in the first six months  of the pandemic  so i think that this is a perfectly  sensible question to ask should we be  following other countries  in using face masks more in school  we know that policy has evolved in this  area but that really is  has got to be one for the medical  experts who advise the government on  these matters  and it's important as far as possible  that they that we follow their advice  and look at the latest evidence that  we've got about for example the way  the virus transmits between young people  and the way that the virus translates  outside school between young people and  older people so we need to follow the  medical advice on this  okay it's going to be a work in progress  as well isn't it okay david thanks for  that  let's move on to our next questions  we've got so many i want to make sure we  get through as many as we can  this is from 13 year old india sethi  given the fact we may be going back to  school in three weeks time  when should our wonderful teachers get  vaccinated  oh long may that enthusiasm for teachers  continue hey rachel  do you think that teachers should be  bumped up the priority list  i think it was entirely appropriate that  the vulnerable the elderly and nhs  frontline workers were prioritized  in the first instance of vaccination and  i know many many of my colleagues do in  teaching as well  but we do feel now in the second phase  that vaccinations should be prioritized  by occupation  there are just under a million teaching  and support staff  in schools in england and i think the  risk of those  people working in crowded schools where  there's very little social distancing  possible and in primary schools where  there is no social distancing between  students and staff  is very different to someone perhaps in  their 50s  who's working from home and is not going  to  expose themselves to a similar type of  risk so for me  to give reassurance to staff in our  schools for whom i feel responsible and  there's a duty of care  i really do think that there should be  some urgent thought  given to um prioritizing teachers and  educational support staff in this  country for vaccination now  um as soon as possible yes i mean it's a  phrase that's used a lot we talked about  it earlier about following the science  certainly  dr ellie cannon the government say that  the jcvi believe that  not profession but age should be the  most important factor when deciding  who gets the vaccine and not just age  but also clinical condition  yes and that's absolutely right but at a  time when  first of all we are doing incredibly  well in terms of the vaccination program  and many people now are being offered  the vaccination around  the age of 50 so we are actually moving  down the groups quite fast in some parts  of the country  and also bearing in mind what we've  already talked about which is  children have given up a huge amount in  the last  year and have been put at risk they've  not been put at risk from  covid thankfully but they've been put at  risk  from loss of physical development  from effects to their mental health  children who are vulnerable in terms of  the support they get from school that's  not been there  so we need to look at ways to mitigate  that risk  now it might be that vaccinating  teachers  if you simply weighed it up against the  other jcvi  groups cannot be justified but we have  to look at vaccinating teachers  holistically in terms of the fact that  getting children back to school now is a  priority  and therefore if there is groundswell  from  teachers to feel more comfortable by  being vaccinated  it's certainly a safe measure then we  should be considering  prioritizing them in order to then  prioritize  schools reopening okay interesting point  thank you very much  it is important to stress i should say  that boris johnson will only be setting  out a plan for schools in england when  he speaks next week there are of course  different timetables  for the different nations of the uk so  as india was saying in her question in  england as it stands  