ansmission in schools vaccination  should be  helping all of this and is already  being shown to reduce severe disease and  hospitalization so i'm optimistic  that having already achieved 17 million  vaccine doses in this country actually  that can only help us get the kids back  to school  okay let's bring in david at this point  and both vaccine questions to you really  whether you think that teachers should  be in line and whether you're worried  about the gap  between the first and the second dose in  terms of kids going back to school  i'm not worried about the strategy of  vaccinating as many people as possible  with the first dose that does seem to be  sensible and based on the medical  evidence  of course there is some risk that by  having children go back before  all of the vulnerable groups have have  had their second vaccination  there is a higher risk than if we shut  things down for much longer  and insisted that everybody had had  their second vaccinations before  young people went back to school but the  problem is that we have to manage the  balance of risks in our society  and there are enormous risks from having  eight or nine million children  out of school for months and months on  end to their future prospects to the  country's prospects  and to their mental health so i think  that what the government is doing  by seeking to reopen on the 8th of march  makes sense  but of course there are some risks  around it and then on the  issue of whether teachers should be  vaccinated as a priority in the next  stage  i agree very much with what rachel said  about the government being right to  start with the older groups and those  with  underlying medical and health conditions  once we get into the second phase of the  roll out of the  of the vaccinations i do think that  there is a case  for looking at prioritizing some of the  groups such as teachers who have to go  out into the workplace at this time and  are more likely to be exposed to the  virus  compared with people like me who are  able to work perfectly happily from  from home i would in a sense prefer that  a teacher was vaccinated in preference  to somebody  who wasn't in a vulnerable group and was  working from home  but medical advisors will need to input  it into that decision with government  and then also need to tell government  whether it's practical  to differentiate between people in other  occupations it's probably quite easy for  the nhs to identify  older people and those with health  conditions and and call them in  whether it's as easy to go through  different high-risk occupations  uh is an issue that we need practical  advice on  from those who are running the national  health service  okay let's get a final thought now find  a couple of thoughts  on well-being and support for young  people  so i know there has been a lot of  anxiety within students about what our  gcse grades are going to be and our  a-level grades  luckily enough for me i had support for  my mental health from action for  children  but i was wondering what schools are  willing to do  to support students as we come out of  does the lockdown feel it's a sensible  approach for schools to include  positive mental well-being for pupils as  a part of the curriculum  perhaps with more outside classes  ah they're lovely perhaps with more  outside classes but there's  a serious point in all of this you know  what could the legacy be from this  pandemic not just in terms  of well-being but you know how the kids  can can learn from it all  to you rachel well i agree  with your speakers i've had some great  ideas um we know that being outside  sport art music dance the social  connection which means that which being  in a school community brings  is really healthy for young people that  well-being should be built into our  school communities not bolted on  and i don't know a head teacher in the  country who isn't thinking really hard  about their well-being strategy for  young people  and i think that will at its center be  about listening to young people in the  way that we have on this program tonight  and actually listening to their worries  so that we're responding  to you know where they are um in their  return to school in this process if we  connect  which we're all as teachers and support  staff really looking forward to  um no teacher enjoys teaching over a  laptop okay  i'm sorry to interact we're just about  to run out of time ellie sorry a final  quick thought from you on that as well  absolutely i mean school is about so  much more than education  it's about mental health physical  activity for some kids it's about  food and avoiding some of the aspects of  poverty  and i think this pandemic should have  really brought that home  to all of us as adults and it absolutely  has to be a priority  coming out of the pandemic okay thank  you so much sorry to rush you all there  but i'm afraid that is  all we have time for on this edition of  covid and the classroom so  all that's left for me to do is thank  our panel this evening  david laws rachel warwick and ellie  cannon thank you very much and also  thank you  to all of our contributors who sent in  their questions from home  and to you for watching us do remember  though we have full coverage of bryce  johnson's announcement on  the way ahead that's right here on sky  news on monday
 

